{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>על</b> — R. Moses said: בתוכה means within Zion, before their exile. In my opinion: בתוכה means in Babylonia. And the proof: על נהרות בבל — for the Arabs [settle] around the river, and since it says נהרות (rivers), plural, this indicates they were many, and there was no river in Jerusalem, for Hezekiah blocked off the Gihon, as written in Chronicles. And yet another proof: that שם refers to Babylonia, for there they said to us, 'Sing us a song.'" "<b>ומלת ותוללינו</b> — is difficult. R. Judah the Grammarian said: it is from the root of על הר גבוה ותלול בדרך רחוקה. R. Moses the Priest said that ותוללינו is from the root יללה, on the pattern of ותושבינו, as if it said יללתינו, and thus he interpreted: ותחת יללתינו היתה שמחה להם. But he did not speak correctly, for there is no mention of ותחת in the verse; and moreover, if יללתינו was joy to them, why would they ask them to sing?! And the great error is that he makes תושבינו an adjective of a person and ותוללינו a noun. In my opinion: we find שרה את אלהים תשתרר עלינו, and also השתרר. Likewise שסוהו כל עוברי דרך — from the class of doubled [roots], and they are שוסים את הגרנות; their root is שסה, and so תלל too — תלה, two roots, and the meaning is one. And so ותוללינו is like תלינו כנורותינו; and ותוללינו = ותליתינו, instruments of joy. And the meaning is doubled, and this was our answer: How can we sing?!"], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 137, :notes {1 "The phrase ותחת יללתינו היתה שמחה להם appears to be Ibn Ezra quoting R. Moses the Priest's interpretation of a related verse (Job 3:7), used as a parallel; retained as he quotes it."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:42:01.988310983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}